# Normalize line endings so behavior is consistent across Windows, Linux,
# Docker, and CI -- this repo is developed on Windows (core.autocrlf=true is
# a common local default there) but built/tested/deployed on Linux (Docker,
# GitHub Actions), so files must be stored with LF in the repository
# regardless of what a given contributor's checkout renders them as.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs. binary; `eol=lf` pins the
# repository (blob) line ending to LF for every text type explicitly
# listed below, while local checkouts may still render CRLF if a
# contributor's own core.autocrlf=true converts on checkout -- that's a
# per-checkout rendering choice, not a repository content difference, so
# it no longer produces the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" diff noise this
# session hit on nearly every git add/commit.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Python
*.py text eol=lf

# Config / data
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.ini  text eol=lf
*.cfg  text eol=lf

# Docs
*.md  text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf

# Shell (must stay LF -- a CRLF shebang line breaks execution on Linux/Docker)
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows PowerShell scripts are conventionally CRLF and are only ever
# executed on Windows -- do not force LF on these.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# SQL / misc text
*.sql text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
.env.canary.example text eol=lf

# Binary -- never touch line endings, avoids corrupting these regardless
# of extension-based heuristics above.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.whl binary
*.pt binary
*.bin binary
*.safetensors binary
*.db binary
